<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Tetrarchy of Galilee and Peraea</h1><p class="st-meta">4 BCE – 39 CE · capital Tiberias</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Herod Antipas's tetrarchy in Galilee and east of the Jordan, the political setting of the ministries of John the Baptist and Jesus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> in 4 BCE, Augustus gave his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a> two separate territories: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peraea">Peraea</a>, a strip east of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hashemite-kingdom-of-jordan">Jordan</a>. Antipas first rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sepphoris">Sepphoris</a>, wrecked in the unrest after his father's death, as his seat.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Antipas</a> ruled as a Roman client and largely respected Jewish sensibilities, minting coins without images. Around 19 CE he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> on the lake shore, named after the emperor, and moved his capital there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> record that he imprisoned and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Luke</a> says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was sent to him during his trial. Fishing, farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> sustained the economy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>His divorce of a daughter of the Nabataean king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aretas-iv">Aretas IV</a> to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodias">Herodias</a> led to a war he lost in 36. Accused of conspiracy by his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Agrippa</a>, he was deposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> in 39 and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. His lands passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-herod-agrippa">Kingdom of Herod Agrippa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 4 BCE – 39 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:59.62%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>4 BCE – 39 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tiberias</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Herod Antipas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tetrarchy of Herod Antipas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=18&amp;focus=tetrarchy-of-galilee-and-peraea" aria-label="Tetrarchy of Galilee and Peraea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5591 986 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tetrarchy of Galilee and Peraea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5911 1242L5908 1277M5911 1242L5304 942M5911 1242L5913 1227"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5908 1277h